## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design practical, data-safe degraded mode in presence of external dependency failures, with observable signals and measurable rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Degraded mode design and safety nets
- Observability signal fusion (metrics, traces, logs)
- Idempotency and transaction safety
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria
- Postmortem and continuous improvement

## Code Example

```javascript
// Idempotent payment handler for degraded mode
async function processPayment(payload) {
  const key = payload.idempotencyKey;
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);
  const res = await gateway.charge(payload);
  await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(res), 'EX', 3600);
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the degraded path in staging?
- How do you ensure no double-charging when the gateway recovers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to triage, choose concrete signals, and perform automated rollback under pressure without customer impact.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant latency metrics (p95/p99)
- Backlog and ingestion lag
- Feature flags and weighted traffic routing
- Grace periods and rollback gates
- Postmortem and runbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-rollback snippet
function rollbackIfNeeded(metrics, thresholds) {
  if (metrics.p95 > thresholds.p95 || metrics.backlog > thresholds.backlog) {
    enableFeatureFlag('NEW_PATH', false);
    routeTraffic(OLD_PATH_WEIGHT = 100);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this rollback plan in a staging environment?
- What telemetry would you collect for the postmortem?
